.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAM-LOGIN 1 "August 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eam-login \- authenticate to Euclid and store a session token
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli
[\fB\-\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR]
.B eam login
.RB { \-\-user | \-u
.I USERID
.BR \-\-password | \-p
.I PASSWORD
|
.BR \-\-oidc | \-o
|
.B \-\-saml
|
.B \-\-onelogin
}
[\fB\-\-namespace\fR|\fB\-n\fR \fINAME\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Sends the given user ID and password to the eam service's
.I login
action, or - with
.B \-\-oidc
or
.B \-\-saml
- authenticates in a browser against the identity provider the
installation is configured with (see those options below). On success, the returned JWT bearer token is saved to
.I $HOME/.euclid/credentials
(see
.BR euclid-cli-eam (1)
for details on that file), so that subsequent
.B euclid-cli eam
commands are authenticated automatically without logging in again.
.PP
If
.B \-\-namespace
is given, it is additionally validated and set as this session's active
namespace, exactly as
.BR euclid-cli-eam-change-namespace (1)
does - every namespace-scoped command run afterward (in any module, not
just eam) is automatically restricted to it via the
.I x\-euclid\-namespace
header, until changed again. Login itself still succeeds even if the
namespace step fails (e.g. an unknown or ungranted namespace); only a
warning is printed and the exit status reflects the namespace failure.
.PP
The full JSON response (containing the token) is also printed to standard
output.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-user " " \fIUSERID\fR
User ID to authenticate as. Required, unless \fB\-\-oidc\fR or
\fB\-\-saml\fR is given.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-password " " \fIPASSWORD\fR
Password for the user. Required for a euclid password login. With
\fB\-\-onelogin\fR it is your OneLogin password and is optional - see
that option for where it is taken from otherwise. Not used with
\fB\-\-oidc\fR or \fB\-\-saml\fR.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-oidc
Authenticate through the configured OpenID Connect provider (e.g.
OneLogin) instead of with a password. No password is asked for or sent.
.IP
The authorization URL is printed and opened in a browser; if no browser
opens - over
.BR ssh (1),
in a container - the printed URL can be pasted into one anywhere, as long
as it can reach both the provider and this machine. The redirect is caught
on a loopback port this process listens on for the length of one login, so
nothing outside this machine can receive the authorization code. Three
minutes are allowed for the browser to come back.
.IP
What is stored afterwards is exactly what a password login stores: the same
kind of token and the same SigV4 access key. A user signing in this way for
the first time is created automatically if the installation is configured
for it (\fIoidc.jit\-provisioning\fR), with no grants - an administrator
still decides what they may do (see
.BR euclid-cli-eam-grant-namespace-access (1)).
.IP
Cannot be combined with \fB\-\-user\fR, \fB\-\-password\fR or
\fB\-\-saml\fR: the identity provider is what says who you are.
.TP
.B \-\-saml
Authenticate through the configured SAML 2.0 identity provider instead of
with a password. No password is asked for or sent.
.IP
Works like \fB\-\-oidc\fR from where you sit - a URL is printed and
opened in a browser - but the return leg differs: the provider posts its
assertion to euclid's assertion consumer service, not to this process, and
euclid then hands the finished session to a loopback listener this process
owns for the length of one login. Nothing about the CLI has to be
registered with the identity provider.
.IP
Cannot be combined with \fB\-\-user\fR, \fB\-\-password\fR or
\fB\-\-oidc\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-onelogin
Authenticate through OneLogin's API rather than a browser, for a login
that has to run unattended.
.IP
euclid-cli identifies itself to OneLogin with the API credential pair from
\fIeuclid.cli.onelogin\fR (Administration → Developers → API Credentials
in OneLogin - not an OIDC client and not a SAML entity), signs you in with
your password and second factor, and posts the assertion OneLogin returns
to euclid's assertion consumer service, which verifies it exactly as it
verifies one that arrived through a browser. Your password and one-time
code go to OneLogin and nowhere else.
.IP
The password is taken from \fB\-\-password\fR if it is given, then from
\fIEUCLID_ONELOGIN_PASSWORD\fR, then from
\fIeuclid.cli.onelogin.password\fR, and is otherwise asked for at the
terminal without echo. Passing it on the command line works but is worth
avoiding where it can be: it is kept in the shell history and is visible in
the process list while the command runs, which the environment variable and
the prompt are not.
.IP
When more than one second factor is enrolled, which of them the code must
come from is taken from \fB\-\-device\fR, then from
\fIeuclid.cli.onelogin.device\fR, and is otherwise asked - listing what
OneLogin offers. This matters more than it looks: a code read from one
authenticator does not verify against another, and what OneLogin says when
that happens ("Failed authentication with this factor") does not mention
the device at all.
.IP
The one-time code, when OneLogin asks for one, is taken from
\fB\-\-otp\fR, then computed from
\fIeuclid.cli.onelogin.otp\-key\fR (or \fIEUCLID_ONELOGIN_OTP_KEY\fR),
and is otherwise asked for at the terminal - naming the device it should
come from. It is asked for at the moment OneLogin wants it rather than up
front, so a code typed here has its full lifetime. Where there is no
terminal to ask at, the login fails saying so rather than waiting.
.IP
An assertion fetched this way answers no request euclid made, so the server
has to accept unsolicited assertions
(\fIeuclid.modules.eam.saml.allow\-idp\-initiated\fR).
.TP
.BR \-\-application " " \fINAME\fR
With \fB\-\-onelogin\fR: which of the configured OneLogin applications
to sign in to, e.g. \fIint\fR or \fIprod\fR. Not needed when
\fIeuclid.cli.onelogin\fR names only one.
.TP
.BR \-\-otp " " \fICODE\fR
With \fB\-\-onelogin\fR: the one-time code. Without it the code is
computed from the configured TOTP secret, or asked for at the terminal. A
code the provider refuses is asked for again, up to three times, without
starting the login over.
.TP
.BR \-\-device " " \fIID\fR|\fITYPE\fR
With \fB\-\-onelogin\fR: which enrolled second factor to use, by device
ID or by type - \fIGoogle Authenticator\fR, or just \fIgoogle\fR; the
type is matched loosely and without regard to case. Only needed where more
than one is enrolled, and only to skip the question. Where two are enrolled
of the same kind, the ID is what tells them apart.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-assertion
With \fB\-\-onelogin\fR: fetch the assertion, print what it says, and
stop without logging in.
.IP
For setting the server up, which is otherwise circular: euclid refuses an
assertion until it has been configured for the provider, and what to
configure it with - the issuer, the audience and the recipient - is written
in the assertion. This prints those three under the settings they belong
to, along with the subject and the attributes available for
\fIsaml.username\-attribute\fR. Nothing is verified: it is what the
document claims, which is exactly what is wanted before there is a
certificate to check it against.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-namespace " " \fINAME\fR
Namespace to make active for this session. Must exist, and the user must
either be an account administrator or hold an explicit grant for it (see
.BR euclid-cli-eam-grant-namespace-access (1)).
Optional; omitted means the session stays unscoped, same as before this
option existed.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Login succeeded, the token was saved, and (if given) the namespace was
set.
.TP
.B 1
Missing/invalid arguments, the server could not be reached, the
credentials were rejected (\fIHTTP 401\fR, "Invalid credentials"), the
response did not contain a token, or (only when \fB\-\-namespace\fR was
given) the namespace step failed - login still succeeded in that case.
With \fB\-\-oidc\fR or \fB\-\-saml\fR: additionally when the identity
provider refused the login, no callback arrived within three minutes, the
callback belonged to a different login attempt, or the installation has no
usable federation configuration.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam login \e
    --user jdoe --password 'hunter2'

euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam login \e
    --user jdoe --password 'hunter2' --namespace production

euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam login --oidc

euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam login --oidc \e
    --namespace production

euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam login --saml

EUCLID_ONELOGIN_PASSWORD=... euclid-cli eam login --onelogin \e
    --application prod
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $HOME/.euclid/credentials
Where the bearer token (and active namespace, if set) is written on
success (mode \fI0600\fR, in a directory created with mode \fI0700\fR if
it doesn't already exist).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eam (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-register (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-change-namespace (1)
